--- Axioms and laws for the time library.
---
--- This is the abstraction contract for `time`: the irreducible interface
--- that every implementation (`time-native` on Rust `std::time`, or a
--- future `time-js`/`time-wasm` backend) must satisfy.
---
--- `time.now_ms` reads the wall clock: milliseconds since the Unix epoch.
--- `time.instant_ns` reads a monotonic clock: nanoseconds from an
--- unspecified epoch, meaningful only as a difference between two readings
--- (`benchmark` uses it for elapsed-time measurement). `time.elapsed_ns(t)`
--- is shorthand for `instant_ns() - t`.
---
--- All three readings are *effectful* — each call may observe a different
--- clock value — so the ops sit behind capabilities in the backend
--- interface (`time-native/time-native.hnl`). The contract itself is
--- pure axiom data.
---
--- Types are symbolic: `Unit` stands for the nullary input `()` — the
--- contract parser has no unit literal, so (like `String`/`Float` in
--- `str.hnl`) it is treated as a type variable. `Int` is a base type.
---
--- Predicate vocabulary available to the contract engine today
--- (`plugin-ext-refinement`'s `Pred`):
---   LengthEq(a, b)   |a| = |b|            LengthLe(a, b)   |a| <= |b|
---   LengthPlusOne    |a| = |b| + 1        LengthSubOne      |a| = |b| - 1
---   Le(a, b)         a <= b               IsPerm(a, b)      a permutes b
---   AllSatisfy(l, p) forall x in l, p(x)  True / False
---   IsSuccess(x) / IsError(x)             &&  ||  ==>
---
--- The ideal time-specific predicates (Monotonic, IsEpochMs,
--- ElapsedEq(output, now - input)) are not expressible yet — they require
--- extending `Pred` in `plugin-ext-refinement`. Each such law is written
--- as a `-- LAW:` comment; the `guarantee:` line states the strongest
--- *parseable* approximation.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Clock reads (effectful — capability-gated in the backend interface)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

axiom time.now_ms : Unit -> Int
-- LAW: output = wall-clock milliseconds since the Unix epoch at call time.
-- LAW: Effectful — successive calls may differ; NOT a pure function.
-- LAW: Non-decreasing only modulo wall-clock adjustments (NTP slew, leap
--      smears, manual clock changes): a backend may observe `now_ms()` go
--      backwards. Use `instant_ns` for elapsed-time measurement.
  guarantee: Le(0, output)

axiom time.instant_ns : Unit -> Int
-- LAW: output = nanoseconds on a monotonic clock from an unspecified,
--      backend-chosen epoch (e.g. process start). Absolute values are
--      meaningless; only differences between readings are.
-- LAW: Monotonic — successive calls are non-decreasing
--      (`instant_ns() <= instant_ns()` in call order). Not expressible in
--      the current `Pred` vocabulary (it is a property of the call
--      sequence, not of a single output); tracked for the `data Pred`
--      refactor in project/BACKLOG.md.
-- LAW: Effectful — successive calls may differ.
  guarantee: Le(0, output)

axiom time.elapsed_ns : Int -> Int
-- LAW: output = instant_ns() - input, i.e. nanoseconds elapsed since a
--      prior `instant_ns` reading. Backends must saturate at 0 when the
--      input lies in the future (bogus or cross-epoch readings must not
--      produce a negative elapsed time).
-- LAW: Effectful — depends on the current clock reading.
  guarantee: Le(0, output)

-- NOTE: `time.sleep_ms` is deliberately absent — it is tracked in
-- project/BACKLOG.md (effectful suspension, not needed by
-- `benchmark`/`property`/`fuzz`).
